1. stationary_sim.py's NetworkSimulator is broken — its constructor is named lebann__init__ instead of __init__, so it's never called and the class is unusable as-is. I used NetworkSimulatorNonStationaryMu(mu_mode="constant", network_density=0.0) instead — it's already used this way elsewhere in the codebase (bayes_grn_non_stationary.py, loss_diagnostic.py) and additionally gives a diagonal A the closed-form OU checks need.
2. Off-by-one in NetworkSimulatorNonStationaryMu.simulate() — the last saved timepoint (index -1) is always all-zero; save_idx tops out one short of save_steps before the loop ends. I sidestepped it by only ever reading data[:, 0, :], but any other caller relying on the final saved point would silently get zeros.

One more thing that shaped the tests: the simulator always initializes cells at mu + small noise, so a plain t0 draw is already near equilibrium — useless for telling true vs. wrong dynamics apart, or for mom_estimate's displacement-based regression. I added draw_perturbed_cross_section (shift the real draw away from mu, e.g. -0.6*mu) for the tests that need visible drift, documented as modeling a freshly-perturbed destructive-measurement population rather than a steady-state one.